docs/research/2026-10-31-reference-context/codex-audit-role-v69-r1.log:1450:+- New (1), checkable from the item: a dated event or step, a figure the item reports first, a forecast or plan with a period, a policy step by a named authority, a specific judgement with a reason ("the CFO said plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity"; "the regulator approved the drug for a second indication"; "the main supplier said its capacity will double by 2027"). Guidance, and a forecast or a plan still in force, count when retold; a retold past actual figure is optional; a development the item does not show to be old is not a retelling. Not new: an arrangement the item only describes as in place, with no new step, even when no date is given ("two airlines fly the planemaker's jets"; "the bank works with a payments firm on cards"); the company's history ("founded in 1998"); a claim everyone following the company makes without a figure and a specific account (that it leads, that demand is strong, that supply is tight: "rivals have spent billions but still lag"); a figure the item itself presents as already known, unless it is guidance or a forecast or plan still in force. Also not new, whoever says it: the company describing what its product does or saves ("runs 40% more units in the same power"; "cuts water use to near zero"); an executive's or author's remark about a trend ("demand is shifting to inference"; "compute is the new constraint"); a named company listed as an example of who uses the product; a comparison with history or with another industry; a reporter's own market-size figure with no source. Restating the sentence in new_in_it does not make it new: new_in_it names the date, step or figure the item is the first to report.
